Circuit Breaker:
    A Circuit Breaker is a protective mechanism that:

    Stops calling a failing downstream service temporarily
    to prevent cascading failures, resource exhaustion, and total system outage.

    💡 Think of it like an electrical circuit breaker in your house.

Why do we need a Circuit Breaker?
    Problem without Circuit Breaker:
        Client
          ↓
        Service A
          ↓
        Service B (slow / down)

If Service B:
    is slow
    times out
    throws errors

Then:
    Threads in Service A get blocked
    Connection pool fills up
    CPU spikes
    Service A also goes down
    Whole system collapses

This is called Cascading Failure ❌

What Circuit Breaker does:
    Client
      ↓
    Service A
      ↓
    Circuit Breaker
       ├─ CLOSED → allow call
       ├─ OPEN → fail fast
       └─ HALF_OPEN → test recovery

       💡 Think of it like an electrical circuit breaker in your house.

What problems Circuit Breaker solves:

    Problem	                        Solved?
    Cascading failure	                ✅
    Thread starvation	                ✅
    Slow downstream	                    ✅
    Network flakiness	                ✅
    Load spikes	                        ⚠️ (combine with rate limiting)


    | Pattern             | Purpose                       |
    | ------------------- | ----------------------------- |
    | **Retry**           | Handle transient failures     |
    | **Circuit Breaker** | Stop calling broken service   |
    | **Rate Limiter**    | Protect from traffic overload |
    | **Bulkhead**        | Isolate resources             |


When NOT to use Circuit Breaker:
    ❌ Inside same JVM
    ❌ Pure in-memory calls
    ❌ Simple CRUD without latency


| Aspect           | Rate Limiter                     | Circuit Breaker           |
| ---------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Protects against | Too many requests                | Failing dependency        |
| Trigger          | Request rate                     | Failure rate / latency    |
| Scope            | Traffic control                  | Dependency health         |
| Fails when       | Limit exceeded                   | Dependency unhealthy      |
| Goal             | Fair usage & overload protection | Prevent cascading failure |


    Circuit Breaker does not depend on Rate Limiter
    Rate Limiter does not depend on Circuit Breaker


    Incoming Request
       ↓
    Rate Limiter
       ↓
    Circuit Breaker
       ↓
    Timeout
       ↓
    Retry (optional)
       ↓
    Downstream Service

 Key takeaway (very important)
        Rate Limiter protects your system from users
        Circuit Breaker protects your system from dependencies


Below is a real, production-grade, end-to-end Circuit Breaker example using Spring Boot + Resilience4j,
exactly how it is done in FAANG / large-scale microservices.

🎯 Real-World Scenario (VERY common)

Order Service → Payment Service
OrderService calls PaymentService
Payment service:
    is slow sometimes
    may return 5xx
    may timeout
We must not cascade failures
We must fail fast when Payment is unhealthy

👉 Circuit Breaker is the correct tool


@CircuitBreaker is ALWAYS applied in the CALLER service, not in the CALLEE service.
So in your example:
    Order Service → calls → Payment Service
    @CircuitBreaker goes in Order Service
    NEVER on /pay API inside Payment Service

Circuit Breaker configuration (YAML):
resilience4j:
  circuitbreaker:
    instances:
      paymentServiceCB:
        registerHealthIndicator: true
        slidingWindowType: COUNT_BASED
        slidingWindowSize: 20
        minimumNumberOfCalls: 10

        failureRateThreshold: 50
        slowCallRateThreshold: 50
        slowCallDurationThreshold: 2s

        permittedNumberOfCallsInHalfOpenState: 3
        waitDurationInOpenState: 30s

        automaticTransitionFromOpenToHalfOpenEnabled: true

        recordExceptions:
          - java.io.IOException
          - java.net.SocketTimeoutException
          - org.springframework.web.client.HttpServerErrorException

        ignoreExceptions:
          - com.example.exception.InvalidPaymentRequestException





@Component
public class PaymentClient {

    private final RestTemplate restTemplate;

    public PaymentClient(RestTemplate restTemplate) {
        this.restTemplate = restTemplate;
    }

    public PaymentResponse charge(PaymentRequest request) {
        return restTemplate.postForObject(
                "http://payment-service/pay",
                request,
                PaymentResponse.class
        );
    }
}



@Service
public class OrderPaymentService {

    private final PaymentClient paymentClient;

    public OrderPaymentService(PaymentClient paymentClient) {
        this.paymentClient = paymentClient;
    }

    @CircuitBreaker(
        name = "paymentServiceCB",
        fallbackMethod = "paymentFallback"
    )
    public PaymentResponse processPayment(Order order) {

        PaymentRequest request = new PaymentRequest(
                order.getOrderId(),
                order.getAmount()
        );

        return paymentClient.charge(request);
    }

    /**
     * FALLBACK MUST MATCH METHOD SIGNATURE + Throwable
     */
    private PaymentResponse paymentFallback(
            Order order,
            Throwable ex) {

        // IMPORTANT: do NOT hide failure
        log.error("Payment service unavailable for order {}",
                  order.getOrderId(), ex);

        // Option 1: graceful degradation
        return PaymentResponse.pending(
                "Payment service temporarily unavailable"
        );

        // Option 2: throw domain exception
        // throw new PaymentServiceUnavailableException();
    }
}

